      "text": "To solve for the real value of \\( v \\) such that \\(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\), we will use the fact that if \\( \\alpha \\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), then \\( a\\alpha^2 + b\\alpha + c = 0 \\).\n\nGiven that \\( \\alpha = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\) is a root, we substitute \\( x = \\alpha \\) into the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ 5\\left(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)^2 + 21\\left(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right) + v = 0. \\]\n\nFirst, simplify the expression inside the equation:\n\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right)^2 = \\frac{(-21-\\sqrt{301})^2}{100} = \\frac{441 + 2 \\cdot 21 \\cdot \\sqrt{301} + 301}{100} = \\frac{742 + 42\\sqrt{301}}{100} = \\frac{371 + 21\\sqrt{301}}{50}. \\]\n\nNow substitute this back into the equation:\n\n\\[ 5 \\left( \\frac{371 + 21\\sqrt{301}}{50} \\right) + 21 \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right) + v = 0. \\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\n\\[ \\frac{5(371 + 21\\sqrt{301})}{50} + \\frac{21(-21 - \\sqrt{301})}{10} + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1855 + 105\\sqrt{301}}{50} + \\frac{-441 - 21\\sqrt{301}}{10} + v = 0. \\]\n\nConvert \\(\\frac{-441 - 21\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) to a common denominator of 50:\n\n\\[ \\frac{-441 - 21\\sqrt{301}}{10} = \\frac{-2205 - 105\\sqrt{301}}{50}. \\]\n\nCombine the fractions:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1855 + 105\\sqrt{301} - 2205 - 105\\sqrt{301}}{50} + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1855 - 2205}{50} + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{-350}{50} + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ -7 + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ v = 7. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( v \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
