      "text": "To find the value of \\( v \\) for which \\(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\), we can use the fact that if \\( r \\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\), then the equation can be written as \\(a(x - r)(x - s) = 0\\), where \\( s \\) is the other root. Here, \\( a = 5 \\) and the roots are \\( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\) and \\( \\frac{-21+\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\).\n\nFirst, we identify the other root:\n\\[\ns = \\frac{-21 + \\sqrt{301}}{10}\n\\]\nThe quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\) can be written as:\n\\[\n5\\left(x - \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)\\left(x - \\frac{-21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right) = 0\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n5\\left(x + \\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)\\left(x + \\frac{21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right) = 0\n\\]\nNext, we expand the product:\n\\[\n5\\left(x^2 + \\left(\\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)x + \\left(\\frac{21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)x + \\left(\\frac{21+\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)\\left(\\frac{21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5\\left(x^2 + \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{10}x + \\frac{441 - 301}{100}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5\\left(x^2 + \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{10}x + \\frac{140}{100}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5\\left(x^2 + \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{10}x + \\frac{7}{5}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5x^2 + \\left(5 \\cdot \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\right)x + 5 \\cdot \\frac{7}{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5x^2 + \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{2}x + 7\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5x^2 + \\left(\\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{2}\\right)x + 7\n\\]\nSince the original quadratic equation is \\(5x^2 + 21x + v = 0\\), we can see that:\n\\[\n21 = \\frac{42 + 2\\sqrt{301}}{2}\n\\]\nSolving for \\( v \\):\n\\[\nv = 5 \\cdot 7 = 35\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\( v \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{35}\n\\]",
